Heh.  I didn't feel up to doing it justice.  Not sure I can now, but
let's see.  I have a lot of nethack experience, including 9 ascensions
in 5 classes (plus a Healer who had a dumb accident in the Plane of
Water), none of which were monks, which usually stalled at some point.
I started playing slashem seriously maybe a couple of months ago, and
have 18 prior deaths on record, in a variety of classes.  I'd had prior
good luck with doppelganger monks, including finding two wizard bones
levels in one game, only to die of attacking a cockatrice with my gloves
off, or paralysis in Grund.  So this is a YAFAP relative to Slash, but
once I was powered up I was in a pretty familiar place.

Game features: lots of altars -- at least 9 in the main dungeon, and
that might be missing one or two.  Very few shops, though -- a weapon
shop and a general store very early on, then just Minetown and Sam.  No
Mall.  No Kobolds.  Minetown temple was friendly.  No bones.  No wands
of cancellation.  No rings of free action except for the one I wished
for in the Castle (good call).  I forget how I got slow digestion; it
took a while, and a wish or stabilized polymorph might have been
involved.

Gameplay itself wasn't too exotic: I went around hitting things.  With a
robe of power (wished, though some were later around) and a helm of
brilliance I eventually got good at it, and had lots and lots of spells.
Identify at 0% failure, along with everything else, except polymorph at
41%.  I got the Gauntlets of Cheesiness relatively early, I think.  A
bag of holding was around somewhere, which often makes me skip Sokoban,
but magic resistance wasn't turning up so I tried it out and got an
amulet of reflection, which is actually pretty good -- no touches of
death are likely before the Castle, so reflecton covers wands, and being
a doppelganger protects your armor from polytraps.

The main use I made of being a doppelganger was not worrying about
polytraps, plus whatever AC bonus they get (along with the monk AC).  I
can't remember if I ever used liquid leap in this game.  I did
eventually polymorph more than I usually do in nethack (almost never)
but not much before I had wands of polymorph and rings of control
anyway.  Xorn to try eating rings and amulets (never worked), to poke
around gems in stone more quickly, to rob Sam blind.  Couatl to dip
potions in water when I didn't have water walking.  Crystal golem for
the hell of it for a short while.  Gargoyle to get the magic lamp from
the Sunless Sea.  Vampire mage to approach wands of draining.

The main point of the game, besides getting first ascension on my
machine (failed, came in a close second in time, far behind in points --
though I tend to think fewer points are better), was to explore Slashem,
and I think I did that well.  I've seen the Mall so didn't miss it, I've
seen the Kobolds, I've seen the Rats.  I'm very disappointed that I saw
no sign of Cthulhu and the Lethe patch.  Otherwise I think I got to see
and clear out everything.  I waited a long time before going back to
Grund; some may remember my posts about wands of draining.  Also about
Vecna; that was this game too.  Of the Quests I did Neutral Quest first,
and the Beholder was a cinch, though I might have gotten lucky in the
deathgaze not going off before I could put a blindfold on.  The Lawful
Quest scared me for a long time -- lots of monsters, and those mind
flayers!  But with a ring of sustain ability and some wands I ventured
it, and discovered it's more boring than anything else.  Nightmare isn't
much, and the flayers are safely locked up unless you want to go cause
trouble.  Even the nymphs aren't much problem since they can't teleport
away with their stolen goods; the biggest risk is falling into lava.
Chaotic Quest was the biggie, because Vecna's mean.  OTOH, having
genocided liches, and being willing to levitate over Elbereth, makes it
almost trivial, once you have the means to hurt him at all.

Frankenstein wasn't that exciting.  Schrodinger's Cat was alive.

I'd never done the Monk Quest before; my memory is dim, but it seems
that Kaen wasn't that hard -- of course, this is with a Slashem monk
with ridiculously low AC and high amounts of damage, and a half-damage
artifact.

Wyrm, spider, giant caves all cleared.  Grund cleared.  Guild was scary
-- someone had a wand of draining, and in general I respect PC monsters
more than others -- but apart from the wand wasn't too bad.  Sunless sea
was fun, since I saw the inscription quickly and got to go looking for
the lamp.  Detect treasure, polymorph, dive.  Temple of Moloch, Lost
Tomb, Ludios all cleared.  Ludios seemed odd -- only baby dragons, not
full ones.  Killing the soldies of Ludios was sped up a lot by using the
Eye of the Beholder.  I think I used it in the Castle as well, and in
the Wizard's Zoo.

I carefully killed Medusa the risky way, without reflection, and Pegasus
came forth, but without a saddle that didn't seem too useful.  I'd hoped
the Wallet of Perseus would show up at this point, but no such luck.

Oh, I just remembered another important use of polymorph: turning into a
black dragon to disintegrate Demogorgon, after several rounds of
attack/cast cure sickness.

Conduct: I tried to be good, but accidentally broke weapons, and would
have on Vecna anyway since I didn't know I just had to be level 16 to
hurt him manually.  I generally kept food conduct, but specifically
broke it for advantages, like telepathy and various resistances.

Wishes: I had a few magic lamps, including one which gave a peaceful
djinn, and possibly one I lost somehow.  The Castle wand started out
with 1 charge.  Oddly, a nightgaunt on the Astral Plane left one with 2
charges behind.  Wished for: cloak of MR, a helm of brilliance, a
replacement helm after the first one evaporated, a ring of free action,
and I don't remember -- perhaps a marker.  The Astral wand I used for a
blessed figurine of a Solar, to ascend with a pet; that didn't work, so
then a scroll of taming, which also didn't work.

I have since verified that my paranoid habit of throwing away tins which
smell "unfamiliar" or "kind of like X" was perfectly justified; those
messages can easily cover a petrifying monster, or even green slime.

The Wizard seemed to resurrect at high frequency at first, but I got
back up through much of the dungeon without seeing him.  I'd gone up and
down a few times, making sure I had fast routes between stairs, since I
knew the Amulet was no-teleport.  Apart from the _ algorithm getting
confused on the demon lord level with iron bars, the endgame run was
fairly uneventful.  Run run run, zap zap zap.  Not much trouble finding
the portals on the elemental planes.

Conclusion: yay, I Ascended!  I don't know if I got a fair sense of
Slashem; there's definitely added risk, in wands and more stoning
monsters, and firearms and grenades; OTOH, a 30th level doppleganger
monk with all resistances, the Gauntlets, 2 blessed healthstones, and
the regen of the Hand of Vecna laughs off almost all threats.  I had 588
max hp at the end (yay, potions); it took hard teamwork for anything to
get me below 550, let alone 500.

IMPORTANT EXCEPTION: Remember that Solar I wanted for a pet?  It did
just fine in kicking my ass; I was at 299 hp when I decided to offer the
Amulet and end it.  I didn't think to check with my stethoscope, so
quite possibly I could have killed him, but he did damage in a way that
Angels, Priests, and PCs didn't.

Astral: once again, the key thing to bring is some wands of death and
lots of wands of teleportation.  Don't fight, zap.  First altar tried
was Pestilence, whose illness was actually scary; also I discovered that
teleporting over his corpses automatically raises him.  That altar was
Neutral; Famine was a wuss, and guarded my temple.  I had a helm of
change alignment with me, so I wouldn't have had to face Death.

I genocided liches early on, and 'h' near the end, mostly to avoid mind
flayers being summoned on Astral.  I hate them.  Though I guess it's
there where losing your memory would matter the least.

There was tons of gold, and I left most of it behind, too heavy.  And
with all those giants, and gem-quality golems, who needs gold?  I spent
a lot at the Oracle, just to see if there was anything new.

So.  I guess this has been long and rambly; sorry for not being more
amusing.

